  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to use a multi-resolution optimization strategy that leverages Fourier analysis to identify and target influential frequency components. This method systematically breaks through symmetries by:\n1. Coarsening the grid to 120 points\n2. Identifying the most influential frequency components through power spectrum analysis\n3. Iteratively optimizing across scales with frequency-based perturbations\n4. Final refinement to the full 600-point resolution with binary enforcement\n\nThis approach specifically addresses the optimization's reliance on greedy local searches that get trapped in narrow minima. The Fourier-based filtering creates structured perturbations that guide the search toward more promising configurations.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Variables: Discrete step function h \u2208 [0,1]^n, n=120 for coarse grid\n- Objective: Minimize C_5 = max_k [np.correlate(h,1-h,'full')*2/n]\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1, \u2211h_i = 240 (for n=600 grid)\n- Derived variables:\n  - Smoothed correlation: exp(alpha*(\u0393_k - max_k \u0393_k))\n  - Power spectrum: |FFT(h)|^2 + |FFT(1-h)|^2\n  - Signal-to-noise ratio: (max_correlation - mean_correlation) / (2*stddev_correlation)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   a) Read initial_h_values (n=600) and store as reference\n   b) Set resolution n0=120\n   c) Convert final configuration to coarse grid:\n      h_coarse = np.interp(np.linspace(0,2,n0),[0,2],h)\n   d) Project to target sum: adjust h_coarse such that \u2211h_coarse \u2248 n0/2 * 1.0 (wait, our dx is 2/n0, so total sum should be n0/2? Actually, constraint is \u2211(dx)\u22481, with dx=2/n0, so \u2211h_coarse = n0/2)\n\n2. Fourier analysis for coarse grid:\n   a) Compute power spectrum: |FFT(h_coarse)|^2 + |FFT(1-h_coarse)|^2\n   b) Sort frequencies by influence (MTI index)\n   c) Select top 15 frequencies contributing most to correlation\n\n3. Multi-resolution optimization loop (time budget 1200 seconds):\n   a) Main optimization using L-BFGS-B with analytical gradients\n   b) Frequency-based restarts every 200 evaluations:\n      i) For selected frequencies, phase shift the amplitude\n      ii) Solve constrained least squares with:\n          objective = -log(sum(exp(alpha*(\u0393_k - M))))\n          constraints: sum(h)=n0/2, 0\u2264h_i\u22641\n   c) Coarse-to-fine interpolation when best candidate found\n\n4. Phase neural network refinement:\n   a) Use simple feedforward NN to approximate optimal correlation\n   b) Train on grid of configurations\n   c) Inject learned patterns into optimization\n\n5. Final refinement to 600 points:\n   a) Use Coiflet wavelets for subband decomposition\n   b) Solve 1-dimensional constrained optimization in each subband\n   c) Re-project to binary space\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Resolution scaling: Support multiple scales (120, 240, 360) selected based on power spectrum\n- Phase shift magnitude: Heuristically between 0-0.5 normalized standard deviation\n- Optimization schedule: 70% of budget to coarse grid, 30% to final refinement\n- Advanced variations:\n  * Bayesian optimization of transition probabilities\n  * Multi-scale early stopping\n  * Pareto optimization for different smoothness weights\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Feasibility checks:\n   - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 \u2200i\n   - \u2211h = n/2 exactly (wavelet refinement ensures)\n   - Original evaluator verification (one final check)\n2. Output:\n   - h_values: 600-point solution\n   - c5_bound: Last evaluated C_5 using np.correlate\n   - n_points: 600\n3. Error handling:\n   - Fallback mechanism: if variance too high, use initial H-values\n   - Calculation safeguards against division by zero\n\nTime allocation:\n- Fourier analysis: 30s\n- Coarse optimization: 650s\n- NN-guided phase: 200s\n- Fine refinement: 320s\n- Validation: 50s\n\nAll implemented using careful numerical differentiation, NumPy/CVXPY with dual-grid architecture.",
